# Generated from: specs/388-support-package-scoped-publication-only-upgrades/requirements.md
Feature: Support package-scoped publication-only upgrades
  Publication repair must represent the operator's exact package authority and
  must not inherit the full repository upgrade's unrelated mutations.

  @SCN001 @regression
  Scenario: Detect exactly one explicitly selected package
    Given several spec packages contain publication rewrites or findings
    When publication-only detection receives exactly one valid package
    Then the report includes only that package
    And zero repeated or multiple selections fail before detection

  @SCN002 @regression
  Scenario: Approval binds exact single-package authority
    Given one selected publication-only package
    When its approval id is calculated
    Then it binds canonical root package and separately globally sorted regular-file and directory inventories
    And each directory record binds one repository-relative POSIX path high-resolution identity and captured deterministic name-type listing
    And reversing raw directory traversal order yields identical records file inventory and approval id
    And it binds exact-string issue digits file digests high-resolution identities and exact rewrite/finding content
    And final apply requires exact directory-record and approval-id equality before target proof

  @SCN003 @regression
  Scenario: Apply performs only selected publication writes
    Given one selected package has approved rewrites and unselected packages are dirty
    When publication-only apply receives the exact approval id and selection
    Then only selected File(s) line payloads change
    And no dependency label backfill GitHub or other upgrade side effect runs

  @SCN004 @regression
  Scenario Outline: Invalid selection fails closed
    Given publication-only detection receives <selection>
    When it validates package authority
    Then it fails before mutation with a stable reason code
    Examples:
      | selection |
      | an empty set |
      | a duplicate path |
      | an absolute or traversal path |
      | a path outside specs |
      | a root whose final component is a symlink |
      | a root with a symlinked ancestor |
      | a symlink hidden lexically before dot-dot |
      | a missing package |
      | a symlinked package |
      | an incomplete package |
      | a wrongly named package |
      | an issue-mismatched package |
      | a non-Approved package |

  @SCN005 @regression
  Scenario Outline: Stale foreign or interrupted apply fails closed
    Given an approved single-package report and a cooperatively honored exclusive root lock
    When apply observes <change> before the final validated boundary
    Then it fails before target rename and does not install unapproved bytes
    And foreign or mutated lock and staged identities detected before pathname cleanup are not overwritten or deleted
    Examples:
      | change |
      | changed source bytes |
      | late package content inserted during directory traversal |
      | same-byte target identity replacement during the authority rerun |
      | same-byte target identity replacement during final target descriptor read |
      | equal-byte non-tasks file replacement during inventory descriptor read |
      | replacement of an empty nested directory with an identical empty listing |
      | replacement of a non-empty directory with identical listing bytes and descendant inode |
      | directory replacement by symlink before recursive inventory traversal |
      | directory replacement by a plain directory during traversal |
      | staged identity replacement during the authority rerun |
      | staged byte mutation during the authority rerun |
      | duplicate task declarations |
      | a different package root or report id |
      | a pre-existing foreign lock |
      | a partial or wrong-byte owner write |
      | a same-token foreign lock replacement |
      | a same-token lock replacement during final lock proof |
      | a same-size lock mutation during final lock proof |
      | an injected atomic rename failure without external interference |

  @SCN006 @regression
  Scenario: Mixed line endings and arbitrary unrelated bytes are preserved
    Given a selected tasks file mixes CRLF and LF and contains invalid UTF-8 byte 0xff
    When publication-only apply succeeds
    Then every original line separator remains in place
    And only approved ASCII declaration-label spans differ
    And byte 0xff and all unrelated files remain byte-for-byte equal

  @SCN007 @regression
  Scenario: PathCast-shaped four-token repair converges
    Given selected T001 through T004 contain four recoverable PathCast-style Files declarations
    And unselected packages contain rewrites and findings
    When the selected item is approved and applied
    Then exactly four selected tokens become canonical File(s) declarations
    And repeating selected detection reports zero writes
    And every unselected package remains unchanged

  @SCN008 @regression
  Scenario: Workflow preserves legacy command resolution before strict parsing
    Given package-bounded publication authority in /sdlc-upgrade-project
    When the workflow renders detection and apply commands
    Then it uses detect-publication and apply-publication with exactly one command token and one spec option
    And strict publication parsing runs only when legacy command-position semantics resolve a publication command
    And a resolved publication command rejects repeated specs ambiguous options values positionals and extra commands before mutation
    And publication-command positionals and command-named option values remain ignored whenever a legacy command resolves

  @SCN009 @regression
  Scenario Outline: Applied cleanup failure remains truthful
    Given the one staged tasks output has atomically replaced its target
    When <cleanup failure> occurs
    Then the stable outcome is publication_files_cleanup_failed
    And state is applied_cleanup_failed with applied true
    And the unproven root lock remains for inspection
    Examples:
      | cleanup failure |
      | exact owned lock unlink returns EACCES |
      | equal-length lock mutation after exact owner bytes are read |

  Rule: Cooperative filesystem boundary
    Authorized nmg-sdlc invocations honor the exclusive root lock.
    Non-target inventory mutation after the completed authority rerun is outside this contract.
    Non-cooperative same-credential mutation or replacement after a target stage or lock
    final proof inside the immediately following pathname syscall gap is undefined external interference.
